    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 041928\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n1228 PM PDT Sun Oct 4 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Above-normal temperatures will continue across the region through \n  Friday, with dangerous heat expected in Death Valley.\n\n* An approaching Pacific trough is expected to bring cooler \n  temperatures, increased winds and low chances of precipitation \n  next weekend.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...through next Sunday.\n\nEnsemble guidance remains in good agreement that unseasonably strong \nridging will remain in place over the southwestern US for much of \nthe upcoming week. High temperatures will average 8 to 12 degrees \nabove normal, while low temperatures will be 5 to 10 degrees above \nnormal. While readings will be hot, HeatRisk for most of the region \nwill remain in the Minor to Moderate categories (levels 1 and 2 out \nof 4). However, areas of Major to Extreme HeatRisk (levels 3 and 4 \nout of 4) are expected in the lower elevations of Death Valley. \nHighs there are expected to reach around 110 degrees, with lows only \nfalling into the mid-80s, offering limited relief from the heat \novernight. As a result, an Extreme Heat Warning is in effect through \n8 PM PDT Friday for elevations below 2000 feet in Death Valley \nNational Park. \n\nBy next weekend, an approaching Pacific trough is expected to begin \naffecting our region. Given that this is nearly a week away, there \nis still quite a bit of uncertainty about what impacts we will see, \nbut the latest ensemble guidance suggests a noticeable cooldown, \naccompanied by gusty southwest winds and low chances of \nprecipitation. The potential for precipitation will depend in part \non the trough's interaction with Hurricane Rachel in the eastern \nPacific and how much moisture it draws into our region. The latest \nNBM shows a 15% chance of precipitation in the Sierra as early as \nSaturday afternoon, with chances ranging from 5 to 20% across much \nof the area Sunday. Some high-elevation snow (above 11000 feet) may \nalso fall in the Sierra in northern Inyo County on Sunday.  \n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 18Z Forecast Package...Light \nwinds following typical daily directional patterns will persist in \nthe terminal area through Monday. The chance of seeing sustained \nspeeds of 10 knots or greater continues to be less than 10 to 20 \npercent, although a random gust to 15 knots cannot be ruled out \nduring peak afternoon heating today and again on Monday. VFR \nconditions will prevail through the TAF period, with only a few high \nclouds with bases AOA 20kft AGL expected during the afternoons.  \n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast \nCalifornia...For the 18Z Forecast Package...Light winds following \ntypical daily directional patterns are expected at most regional TAF \nsites through Monday. Two exceptions will be the northern Owens \nValley, including KBIH, where southerly winds gusting as high as 25 \nknots are expected this afternoon, and the western Mojave Desert, \nincluding KDAG, where easterly wind gusts to around 30 knots are \npossible this afternoon and early evening. VFR conditions will \nprevail through Monday in all areas, with only a few high clouds \nwith bases AOA 20kft AGL expected. \n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Planz\nAVIATION...Planz\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
